1
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:06,970
There were two of them.

2
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:09,840
It must be them again.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:16,940
Iron Fist Ketil

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,100
Their footprints do look similar.

5
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,560
Why'd this have to happen
while the master's gone?

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:18,900
What's the damage?

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:23,460
One bag of flour and some dried meat.

8
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:27,570
It's no use, boss.

9
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:31,430
They're all mixed up with
other footprints. I can't track them.

10
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:33,120
No matter.

11
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:37,910
They don't steal much,
but they've developed a taste for it.

12
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,880
Do they think we don't know, Snake?

13
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:47,680
Fine, then.
Sounds like they need to be punished.

14
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,790
What? That's incredible.

15
00:02:29,790 --> 00:02:32,870
That means the old master likes you.

16
00:02:34,870 --> 00:02:36,530
Does he?

17
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:38,090
Yes, he does.

18
00:02:38,090 --> 00:02:40,230
Not only does he
lend you his tools and horse,

19
00:02:40,230 --> 00:02:42,590
but he invited you to eat
at the same table with him.

20
00:02:42,590 --> 00:02:46,460
He's just paying us back
for doing his chores.

21
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:50,410
We're slaves.

22
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:53,020
Normally, people don't pay us back.

23
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:56,090
Oh, right.

24
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:02,860
Now that you mention it,
I guess you're right.

25
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:04,860
That's right.

26
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:09,120
But why does he like us, then?

27
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,870
Huh? U-Uh...

28
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:15,480
I'm sorry.

29
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:18,630
Einar, you're a good person.

30
00:03:18,630 --> 00:03:20,300
You just don't realize it yourself.

31
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:30,290
What? No, wait, but...

32
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:33,020
Einar, I'm heading out.

33
00:03:30,910 --> 00:03:34,010
I'm not that special.

34
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:37,390
Huh? Oh, sure.

35
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:41,760
Is he trying to be considerate?

36
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:48,730
He's a good guy, too.
Doesn't talk much, though.

37
00:03:48,730 --> 00:03:49,840
I know.

38
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:51,530
Arnheid!

39
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:54,150
Oh, no. It's the mistress.

40
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:56,330
Sorry for keeping you tied up.

41
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:57,740
Not at all.

42
00:03:57,740 --> 00:04:02,500
I look forward to every morning
now that I have someone to talk to.

43
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,720
See you later.

44
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:09,540
See you tomorrow morning.

45
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,760
Yahoo!

46
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:19,180
She called me a good person.

47
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:22,480
I'm a good person!

48
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:32,280
How dare you stay out there chatting
when breakfast isn't even ready yet.

49
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,550
I'm sorry, mistress.

50
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:37,450
We don't keep you around for fun, you know.

51
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:39,940
You have to earn your keep.

52
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:41,790
Yes, mistress.

53
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:45,670
The master will be returning
from the thing today.

54
00:04:45,670 --> 00:04:50,670
He might be bringing guests back with him,
so prepare accordingly.

55
00:04:52,670 --> 00:04:58,060
I suppose an enslaved man would be
a good match for an enslaved woman.

56
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,250
Hey, they're sprouting.

57
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:05,310
These are wheat seedlings, Thorfinn.

58
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:10,940
They look so... feeble.

59
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:13,300
Do they always look like this?

60
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:15,740
Yup, they sure do.

61
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:22,700
How strange. So these become bread?

62
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:24,650
Surprised?

63
00:05:24,650 --> 00:05:25,410
Yeah.

64
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:27,830
Impressive, isn't it?

65
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:31,140
Yeah, it's amazing.

66
00:05:31,710 --> 00:05:35,550
We'll grow and sell, grow and sell,

67
00:05:35,550 --> 00:05:38,220
and eventually buy ourselves back.

68
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:41,020
It might takes years, but someday...

69
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,650
All right. I'm feeling motivated.

70
00:05:47,650 --> 00:05:50,450
This is our first step toward freedom.

71
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:55,020
Let's clear these woods,
so we can plant double next year.

72
00:05:57,520 --> 00:05:58,330
Oh...

73
00:06:00,030 --> 00:06:06,290
We can eventually buy our freedom by farming,

74
00:06:06,810 --> 00:06:09,930
but I wonder if Arnheid has a similar deal.

75
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:16,280
I don't know. Why don't you ask her?

76
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:21,780
You think it'd be okay to ask that?

77
00:06:22,810 --> 00:06:25,870
More importantly, what are we
doing with these today?

78
00:06:25,870 --> 00:06:30,190
Oh, the wheat?
We won't have anything to do for a while.

79
00:06:30,810 --> 00:06:33,730
I see. So we should keep clearing the woods.

80
00:06:33,730 --> 00:06:35,570
So we'll pray.

81
00:06:35,570 --> 00:06:37,320
What? Pray?

82
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:45,040
Almighty God!

83
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,870
Make our crops grow!

84
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:48,910
Make them ripen and grow!

85
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:50,500
Keep insects and mold away!

86
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:54,250
Send the rain our way!

87
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,370
Join me.

88
00:06:58,370 --> 00:06:59,470
What?

89
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:04,220
Who am I supposed to p-pray to?

90
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:06,130
Anyone.

91
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:11,060
Yahweh, Freya...
Pray to everyone and everything.

92
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:14,580
Get down here.

93
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:22,470
If we want a successful harvest,
all we can do is pray to God.

94
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:24,010
We have nothing to offer.

95
00:07:24,010 --> 00:07:26,830
But we'll pray extra hard to make up for it.

96
00:07:27,490 --> 00:07:30,170
Make our crops grow!

97
00:07:30,170 --> 00:07:32,310
Come on. Put your hands
together and pray with me.

98
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:33,740
What? O-Okay...

99
00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:38,590
M-Make our crops grow...

100
00:07:38,590 --> 00:07:40,090
I can't hear you!

101
00:07:40,090 --> 00:07:41,220
Okay!

102
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,010
Make our crops grow!

103
00:07:43,010 --> 00:07:44,290
Don't be so embarrassed.

104
00:07:44,290 --> 00:07:46,970
Nothing's more embarrassing
than doing it half-assed.

105
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:48,550
O-Okay!

106
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:49,830
Ready, and...

107
00:07:49,830 --> 00:07:51,730
Keep insects and mold away!

108
00:07:51,730 --> 00:07:55,480
Send the rain our way!

109
00:08:07,210 --> 00:08:11,880
Wow, the farm hasn't changed at all.

110
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:16,390
Are Mom and Olmar doing well, Dad?

111
00:08:16,390 --> 00:08:20,750
Yeah. Olmar is so full of energy,
he's a pain in my ass.

112
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:23,880
He's anxious to head off to battle like you.

113
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:29,450
He hasn't changed since he was little either,

114
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:31,430
even though he's a coward.

115
00:08:31,430 --> 00:08:35,060
Everyone has their strengths
and weaknesses, Thorgil.

116
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:37,290
He's not fit for battle.

117
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:40,900
People should stay where they belong.

118
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:44,530
I wouldn't be so sure.

119
00:08:44,530 --> 00:08:48,580
Sometimes the battlefield changes a man,
like King Canute.

120
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:50,030
"King"?

121
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:53,130
Nobody calls him a prince anymore.

122
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:56,570
He changed quite a lot in this war.

123
00:08:56,570 --> 00:08:59,320
He's a proper general now.

124
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:04,430
Why not send Olmar to the battlefield
and see how he fares?

125
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:06,850
You can't be serious.

126
00:09:06,850 --> 00:09:08,900
He'd never survive with his skills.

127
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:16,430
Isn't that the master?

128
00:09:16,430 --> 00:09:18,700
Perfect timing.

129
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:22,150
Isn't that...

130
00:09:22,610 --> 00:09:26,030
It really is you, Thorgil.

131
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:28,490
When did you get back?

132
00:09:28,490 --> 00:09:30,160
Five or six days ago.

133
00:09:30,650 --> 00:09:35,080
I ran into Dad at the thing,
so I thought I'd stop by.

134
00:09:35,590 --> 00:09:38,580
You're a tough one. You haven't died yet?

135
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:42,840
Thanks to the training
you put me through, Snake.

136
00:09:43,310 --> 00:09:46,220
Snake, what have you got trailing behind you?

137
00:09:46,710 --> 00:09:47,970
Oh, right.

138
00:09:47,970 --> 00:09:49,380
Hey.

139
00:09:50,930 --> 00:09:53,390
Come on, hurry up.

140
00:09:55,430 --> 00:09:56,950
They're thieves.

141
00:09:56,950 --> 00:09:59,450
I was going to hand them over to you.

142
00:09:59,450 --> 00:10:00,690
Thieves?

143
00:10:00,690 --> 00:10:04,870
We'd been experiencing a rash
of similar burglaries recently.

144
00:10:04,870 --> 00:10:09,000
I've got a hunch they're behind all of them.

145
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:15,580
They may be kids, but theft is theft.

146
00:10:15,970 --> 00:10:19,800
They need to be punished, master.

147
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:44,280
Sture, my hands hurt.

148
00:10:45,510 --> 00:10:47,820
What's going to happen to us?

149
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:54,300
Are we going to die?

150
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:59,460
It's okay. It's all right.

151
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,220
I'm right here with you.

152
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:06,960
In other words...

153
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:09,510
people talk about military units,

154
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:12,590
but nobody really cares who their
allies or enemies are supposed to be.

155
00:11:13,210 --> 00:11:18,230
Once the fighting starts, you and your
clansmen pick your targets and attack.

156
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:22,160
Everyone's desperate to prove they're
the greatest daredevil on the North Sea.

157
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:27,610
After the battle,
you enjoy plundering the spoils.

158
00:11:27,610 --> 00:11:31,450
Guys kill each other all the time
over women they can sell.

159
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:33,880
Olmar.

160
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:34,830
Huh?

161
00:11:34,830 --> 00:11:38,350
What do you say?
Do you want to go to England?

162
00:11:38,350 --> 00:11:40,010
Uh, I...

163
00:11:40,580 --> 00:11:42,830
What? You don't want to?

164
00:11:42,830 --> 00:11:46,130
Oh, o-o-of course I'll go to war!

165
00:11:46,130 --> 00:11:48,200
I'd love a chance to prove myself!

166
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:51,340
That's my little brother!

167
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:49,640
Ow...

168
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:54,770
Maybe I'll give you a souvenir.

169
00:11:54,770 --> 00:11:56,830
Bring me my necklace.

170
00:11:56,830 --> 00:11:57,580
Yes, sir.

171
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:09,160
What is this, jerky? Can I eat it?

172
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:13,410
Sure, but ears don't taste very good.

173
00:12:13,410 --> 00:12:14,490
Huh?

174
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:17,740
They're the ears of Englishmen.

175
00:12:18,150 --> 00:12:20,660
Ears?

176
00:12:31,590 --> 00:12:33,030
Oh, uh...

177
00:12:33,030 --> 00:12:35,270
You really are a scaredy-cat.

178
00:12:35,270 --> 00:12:37,320
How are you going to fight like that?

179
00:12:38,850 --> 00:12:42,280
We're sons of Iron Fist Ketil.

180
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:44,140
Iron...

181
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:45,980
Iron Fist?

182
00:12:45,980 --> 00:12:47,910
You don't know?

183
00:12:47,910 --> 00:12:50,480
Our dad was a legend when he was young.

184
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:58,240
As soon as the fighting started, his weapon
would shatter under his own strength,

185
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:02,370
so he'd fight the rest
of the battle bare-handed.

186
00:13:02,370 --> 00:13:05,900
He accomplished more than
anyone fighting like that.

187
00:13:08,790 --> 00:13:12,420
What? Our dad?

188
00:13:12,420 --> 00:13:17,070
When I talk to the veterans,
the name Iron Fist comes up a lot.

189
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:19,660
You should show more respect to Dad.

190
00:13:21,810 --> 00:13:24,990
Thorgil, you must be tired from your journey.

191
00:13:24,990 --> 00:13:27,400
Why don't you rest inside?

192
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:30,150
I'm not that weak.

193
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:31,500
Thorgil.

194
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:40,410
If the great Iron Fist Ketil commands it.

195
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:42,540
Master.

196
00:13:44,290 --> 00:13:49,620
I'm sorry, but could you take care of
those two before you go off to rest?

197
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:02,120
First, why don't you tell me your names?

198
00:14:04,340 --> 00:14:06,240
Come on, tell him your names.

199
00:14:06,810 --> 00:14:08,470
Don't do that.

200
00:14:08,470 --> 00:14:10,970
They can't speak because they're frightened.

201
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:12,020
Sorry.

202
00:14:14,420 --> 00:14:16,950
Tell me your names.

203
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:24,080
I-I'm Sture. My younger sister's name is Thora.

204
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:28,470
I see, I see. Sture and Thora.

205
00:14:28,470 --> 00:14:31,330
Do you have anyone who could
speak in your defense?

206
00:14:31,870 --> 00:14:35,210
Besides them, their family consists of
their mother and a baby.

207
00:14:35,710 --> 00:14:39,750
Their mother was sick in bed,
so I didn't bring her here.

208
00:14:39,750 --> 00:14:41,880
Their father is missing.

209
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:45,850
What's your father's name? Is he well?

210
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:49,350
Answer him.

211
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:53,660
My d-dad is Snorri.

212
00:14:54,370 --> 00:15:02,170
Last fall, he went out to sell vegetables
and never came back.

213
00:15:03,170 --> 00:15:08,080
Is your grandfather's name by any chance Bo?

214
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:10,960
Yes.

215
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:15,210
Their father is Snorri, son of Bo.

216
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:18,530
He owes money on the land
he rents from you.

217
00:15:18,530 --> 00:15:21,330
Ah, that Snorri.

218
00:15:21,330 --> 00:15:23,820
I knew I hadn't seen him recently.

219
00:15:24,470 --> 00:15:26,670
He must be dead.

220
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:28,630
He hasn't come back in a year.

221
00:15:28,630 --> 00:15:30,100
He's not coming back.

222
00:15:30,590 --> 00:15:32,770
A thief probably killed—

223
00:15:32,770 --> 00:15:35,570
You're wrong! Dad's just out on an errand!

224
00:15:44,010 --> 00:15:46,750
Traditionally, families that have
lost their breadwinner

225
00:15:46,750 --> 00:15:50,960
are hired by other families to do work.

226
00:15:51,430 --> 00:15:56,790
These children may be healthy,
but they have a sick mother and a baby.

227
00:15:56,790 --> 00:15:58,750
Nobody would take them in.

228
00:15:59,970 --> 00:16:02,260
What's the matter, honey?

229
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:07,120
J-Just got a bit of dust in my eye.

230
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:15,110
So, what did these two steal?

231
00:16:15,110 --> 00:16:21,670
Their crimes are numerous, but they
confessed to stealing one bag of flour.

232
00:16:21,670 --> 00:16:24,160
My men and I can attest to that.

233
00:16:24,570 --> 00:16:27,280
You didn't threaten them
into saying that, did you?

234
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:32,540
No, of course not.
Though I did convince them to talk.

235
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:40,420
I'll wait until this bag is empty.

236
00:16:40,890 --> 00:16:44,360
You'll get off easy as long as you're honest.

237
00:16:46,610 --> 00:16:52,080
I told them your name's written
on the bottom of our bags.

238
00:16:54,250 --> 00:16:56,350
That's practically threatening them!

239
00:16:56,350 --> 00:17:02,070
What? That wouldn't have been enough to
make them confess if they weren't guilty.

240
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:06,320
Then it's simple. One arm.

241
00:17:09,490 --> 00:17:14,080
M-My sister's still little!
Please go easy on her!

242
00:17:14,550 --> 00:17:19,790
Oh? Then you'll accept
your sister's punishment, too.

243
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:21,840
We'll cut off both your arms.

244
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:25,900
No... Sture...

245
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:34,810
Well... I know what I said, but I'd like
to ask you to go easy on them, too.

246
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:40,440
Then it's decided. Right, Dad?

247
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:45,420
He's horrifying.

248
00:17:45,950 --> 00:17:48,420
I can hardly believe he's my son.

249
00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:53,950
Why did you have to steal from me?

250
00:17:54,630 --> 00:17:59,430
I have no choice but to punish you.

251
00:18:02,330 --> 00:18:04,050
I'm against it.

252
00:18:04,050 --> 00:18:06,650
You'd be practically executing him.

253
00:18:06,650 --> 00:18:09,390
It's too great a punishment for theft.

254
00:18:09,390 --> 00:18:10,540
Pater...

255
00:18:11,150 --> 00:18:15,230
May I offer my opinion, master?

256
00:18:16,740 --> 00:18:21,230
Yes. Yes, speak, Pater.

257
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:26,250
As one who helps run this farm,

258
00:18:26,250 --> 00:18:30,450
I don't think it's right to harshly
punish such a young boy.

259
00:18:30,450 --> 00:18:33,870
Cutting off both his arms
would make him useless.

260
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:37,370
Sture, how old are you?

261
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:39,870
Twelve.

262
00:18:39,870 --> 00:18:44,360
Good. That's old enough
to start working with the adults.

263
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:50,770
You must be compensated for their theft,
and their father's debt must be repaid.

264
00:18:50,770 --> 00:18:54,250
They can pay you back by working.

265
00:18:54,250 --> 00:19:00,900
I recommend that you hire these children
and their mother to work on your farm.

266
00:19:01,410 --> 00:19:08,150
They can grow crops on the
land you lent to their father.

267
00:19:08,150 --> 00:19:11,370
I'll teach Sture what to do.

268
00:19:11,370 --> 00:19:16,790
If he grows into a good worker,
you'll be fully compensated for this matter.

269
00:19:17,560 --> 00:19:20,550
They won't need to steal anymore, either.

270
00:19:20,550 --> 00:19:22,530
It solves the problem.

271
00:19:23,430 --> 00:19:25,910
Yes, yes. I couldn't agree more.

272
00:19:25,910 --> 00:19:28,920
I must be compensated
for the loss, yes. In that case—

273
00:19:28,920 --> 00:19:34,930
For the aforementioned reasons, I believe a
beating would be an appropriate punishment.

274
00:19:39,220 --> 00:19:40,320
What?

275
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:44,080
Well, I understand your argument.

276
00:19:45,820 --> 00:19:51,670
No. No, wait. They can pay back
what they stole by working for me.

277
00:19:51,670 --> 00:19:54,780
Paying off their debt
can be their punishment.

278
00:19:56,160 --> 00:20:01,140
They can't get off with
no punishment at all, master.

279
00:20:01,690 --> 00:20:05,460
If you don't, people won't take us seriously.

280
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:14,740
And nobody likes a kid with sticky fingers.

281
00:20:15,590 --> 00:20:18,390
Punishing them is for their own good.

282
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:27,160
How many strokes will it be?

283
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:39,900
Five... Ten. Ten strokes.

284
00:20:40,790 --> 00:20:42,860
That's ten strokes each.

285
00:20:44,180 --> 00:20:47,400
You're first, boy. Bite down hard.

286
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:50,690
Sture...

287
00:20:50,690 --> 00:20:52,090
Stay back!

288
00:20:52,090 --> 00:20:53,130
Sture!

289
00:20:53,130 --> 00:20:56,990
Twenty! Hit me 20 times!

290
00:20:56,990 --> 00:21:00,220
Just please, please don't hit my sister!

291
00:21:02,730 --> 00:21:06,350
Oh? Now this is interesting.
You've got some guts.

292
00:21:06,790 --> 00:21:09,230
Dad, let me do it.

293
00:21:09,630 --> 00:21:13,210
What? But you don't know how to hold back.

294
00:21:12,120 --> 00:21:14,380
N-No...

295
00:21:13,210 --> 00:21:14,510
Don't kill him.

296
00:21:14,510 --> 00:21:15,360
Yeah, yeah.

297
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:16,830
Be gentle.

298
00:21:16,830 --> 00:21:17,890
Yeah, yeah.

299
00:21:17,890 --> 00:21:19,910
Don't hit his head or anything.

300
00:21:19,910 --> 00:21:21,370
Okay, okay.

301
00:21:21,370 --> 00:21:25,580
Gentle, right? Here we go, kid.

302
00:21:31,410 --> 00:21:32,420
Sture!

303
00:21:35,990 --> 00:21:37,650
One.

304
00:21:36,980 --> 00:21:39,010
Sture!

305
00:21:37,650 --> 00:21:40,050
W-Wait. Stop, Thorgil.

306
00:21:40,050 --> 00:21:41,920
I'll do it. I'll do it myself.

307
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:11,820
Where's Dad?

308
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,350
He went to bed.

309
00:22:14,350 --> 00:22:18,720
Good grief. Iron Fist Ketil
must be getting old.

310
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:26,160
All that Iron Fist stuff is a lie.

311
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:32,940
A Norse man can't be a coward
and make it in this world.

312
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:40,420
Everyone expects me to be brave.

313
00:22:45,200 --> 00:22:53,700
Nobody suspects a "great man"
like me would lie about that.

314
00:23:00,390 --> 00:23:05,300
I'm afraid of Thorgil, my own son.

315
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:10,860
I'm afraid of war. I'm afraid of violence.

316
00:23:14,840 --> 00:23:19,550
Being kind can't possibly be a bad thing.

317
00:23:20,640 --> 00:23:24,530
I know your pain.

318
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:30,300
You're the only one who'd say that to me.

319
00:23:31,050 --> 00:23:36,540
You're the only one I can tell everything.

320
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:41,340
Stay with me.

321
00:23:43,090 --> 00:23:50,220
Without you, my world is full of darkness.

322
00:25:36,370 --> 00:25:39,880
An Empty Man

323
00:25:37,330 --> 00:25:39,040
"An Empty Man."
